    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1298">

                <pb facs="1271" n="1261" sameAs="frus:frus1949v09/pg1261"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1115/7–549: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Canton</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Scott</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Canton</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-07-05">July 5,
                        1949</date>.<lb/>[Received July 6—12:56 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>218. Eighteen Americans, all missionaries, now resident Kulangsu (Amoy) will
                    remain except for family of four soon departing on transfer to Manila. Those in
                    up-country stations also said not evacuating; some visiting Amoy for vacations.
                    Two American male employees Civil Air Transport, families already evacuated,
                    will leave if situation worsens.</p>
                <p>Consular posters left with Dr. Clarence Holleman, informal head of Colony. Group
                    reports no problems; funds sufficient; relations local authorities very
                    good.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Scott</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
